narrative:
## Wearing Many Hats

A single type can satisfy multiple interfaces. Each interface represents one capability. Together, they describe everything the type can do.

### Three Interfaces

```xiom
interface Greetable {
  fn greet() -> Str;
}

interface Displayable {
  fn display() -> Str;
}

interface Comparable {
  fn is_older_than(other: &Self) -> Bool;
}
```

### Person Satisfies All Three

```xiom
type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
}

fn Person.greet(self) -> Str {
  return "Hi, I'm " + self.name;
}

fn Person.display(self) -> Str {
  return self.name + " (" + string.int_to_str(self.age) + ")";
}

fn Person.is_older_than(self, other: &Person) -> Bool {
  return self.age > other.age;
}
```

### Functions with Multiple Constraints

```xiom
fn introduce[T: Greetable + Displayable](thing: &T) {
  io.println(thing.greet());
  io.println("  Details: " + thing.display());
}
```

`[T: Greetable + Displayable]` means T must satisfy BOTH interfaces. Inside the function, you can call both `.greet()` and `.display()`.

### Using the Multi-Interface Functions

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let alex = Person{ name: "Alex", age: 25 };
  let bob = Person{ name: "Bob", age: 30 };

  introduce(&alex);
  io.println("Alex older than Bob: " + string.bool_to_str(alex.is_older_than(&bob)));
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Multiple Interfaces Beat One Big Interface

Bad approach: one `PersonAbilities` interface with greet, display, and compare.
Good approach: three focused interfaces.

Why? Because you might need a function that only needs Displayable (a logger) but NOT Greetable. With a big interface, you force every user to implement everything. With small interfaces, you only require what you actually use.

This principle is called the **Interface Segregation Principle**: many small, focused interfaces are better than one large, unfocused one.

